WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Film Courage: Forgive me, Michael.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.889
Can you explain please quickly what a structural
device is?

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:06.109
It’s a little unclear to me.

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:07.740
Michael Hauge: Sure.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.630
Well, first of all let’s talk about what
structure means.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.940
Structure or plot structure simply means the
sequence of events.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.960
What’s the sequence?

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.789
What happens and when does it happen?

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:23.669
Let’s say you’re writing a script and
you want a car chase in it.

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:24.900
Is it going to open the movie?

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.470
Is it going to be right at the midpoint?

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.769
Is it going to be the climax?

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:30.259
Where does it occur?

00:00:30.259 --> 00:00:33.360
That’s a structural question.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.550
So what we’ve been talking about extensively
is my approach to the overall approach to

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:44.210
the story which are the six stages which are
created by those five key turning points.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.790
But within each stage, you still have to be
smart about what the sequence of events would

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:50.790
be.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:58.390
There are some other structural tools you
can use to help you maximize the emotional

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.560
experience for the audience.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.960
Let me give a couple of examples.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.579
One of those would be anticipation.

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:11.179
Keep in mind that when we go to the movies
what we like to do is try and guess what is

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:12.179
going to happen next.

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:14.909
We’re always thinking about “Oh, what
is going to happen?”

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:19.289
And in the movie, you want to create anticipation
in the movie.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:22.630
And if you go to see JAWS….[laughs] Film
Courage: [Laughs] This is now the third time

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:26.149
during our interview where I think of a movie
[quietly to myself] and you say it!

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:31.890
Michael Hauge: Well, JAWS is such a classic
example of anticipation but it was sort of,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.479
because of the necessity of having a broken
shark, all they really had was a dorsal fin.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:40.770
But every time we see the dorsal fin, we’re
anticipating “Oh, my God!

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.840
Here comes the shark.”

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.579
In fact, this has nothing to do with the screenplay
but [composer] John Williams added a lot of

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:50.979
anticipation by creating a theme just for
the shark and now all you have to do is hear

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:52.590
Da…Da…[mimics the shark theme from JAWS].

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.630
Everybody in the world knows what those two
notes mean and so that is anticipation.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:03.560
So we love that because we don’t just want
to sit there bored and only enjoy emotion

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:04.630
when it happens.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.489
We want to think about the conflict to come
because emotion grows out of conflict.

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:10.679
So that is anticipation.

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:16.840
To create that anticipation, there is another
device that is really effective to use and

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.420
that is what is known as superior position.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.840
Superior position means telling your reader
or your audience something that some of the

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.610
characters in the movie don’t know.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.959
Because that let’s your audience or your
reader anticipate what is going to happen

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:39.240
when the people who don’t know that this
is happening or this exists, don’t know

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.150
what I know find out what happened, what I
know.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:50.799
So the classic example of that would be in
a suspense thriller and then that means you

00:02:50.799 --> 00:02:51.970
go to Alfred Hitchcock.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.680
He talked about this all the time.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.420
He had an anecdote he use to say and that
is if you’re shooting a film and two people

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.709
are talking sitting across from a desk.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:06.599
And all at once a bomb that was hidden in
the desk goes off and they’re blown to bits,

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:08.010
you would say (and I’m paraphrasing.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:14.550
He said it much more elegantly) you’d would
probably squeeze about 60 seconds of emotion

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.200
out of that because you’d be shocked and
surprised.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.489
It would be awful and then the audience is
saying “Okay, now what?”

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:27.010
But he says “Supposed the camera cut to
the bomb inside the desk and we know it’s

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.010
there.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.519
But the two people at the desk don’t know.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.110
Then you could milk that for 10 minutes because
then you’re thinking “Where is the bomb

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.219
squad?

00:03:35.219 --> 00:03:36.790
Get out of the room?

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:37.930
Get out of the room?”

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:43.730
And because we know that the bomb is there
but the two people at the desk don’t know.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:49.459
We have superior position and with that superior
position we can anticipate what’s going

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:56.450
to happen and anticipating the horrible violence
and we don’t want it to happen and now we

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:02.500
are deeply emotionally involved for that conflict
for a for a much longer period of time.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.950
And if you want a more recent example of that
(I’m not going to say too much for the sake

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.760
of those who haven’t seen it) but I strongly
recommend everyone see EYE IN THE SKY.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:16.729
And all I will say about that is for a great
example of superior position, we know what

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:21.079
is going to happen but the girl selling bread
doesn’t know.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:29.120
And that creates tension that lasts in the
movie for at least (I would guess) 30 to 45

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.120
minutes.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.740
Because that whole movie in a way is built
on that superior position and anticipation

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:47.610
of conflict A third example of a structural
device that I recommend highly is a ticking

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.110
clock.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.020
That means you always want to built conflict
in your story.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:57.981
You can exponentially increase the level of
conflict by having whatever you’re trying

00:04:57.981 --> 00:05:01.720
to have the hero do, be a race against time.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.660
So again if you have a thriller, it’s like
they’re rushing to stop the killer before

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.110
the killer blows up the bomb in the cathedral.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:19.980
Or there is rushing to stop the bad guy before
the bad guy kills the kidnap victim that they’re

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.169
holding hostage.

00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:26.970
Okay, so there is a lot of examples of it
in action movies and thrillers and also there

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.880
are a lot of examples of it in romantic comedies.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.400
If you look closely at romantic comedies you’ll
see often in the last act of the movie right

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.160
before the climax, there will be a scene which
is what I call the race to the wedding.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:45.200
And you’ll see actually the pace speeds
up.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.610
The hero is rushing to get to the love interest.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:55.979
So in the movie KNOTTING HILL, she comes to
him and says that famous line “I’m just

00:05:55.979 --> 00:06:01.259
a girl standing in front of a boy asking him
to love her and wants to be with him (the

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:07.330
Julia Roberts character) and Will (the Hugh
Grant character) says “I have to refuse

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.030
because I can’t have my heart broken again.”

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.380
He’s retreating in his identity.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.920
So then he goes to his friends and says “Did
I do the right thing?”

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.649
And all his friends say “Yeah, yeah, that’s
right.

00:06:18.649 --> 00:06:20.540
It would be a bad idea” and so on.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:28.379
Until his reflection character Spike comes
into the room and they all tell him he just

00:06:28.379 --> 00:06:34.581
broke up with Anna Scott and he looks at Hugh
Grant and says “You daft prick!” because

00:06:34.581 --> 00:06:40.199
that’s him holding the hero’s feet to
the fire saying you’re not going with your

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:41.199
truth.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:42.389
What then happens?

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:48.410
We know and Hugh Grant knows, she’s about
to leave town and he has no idea how to reach

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.979
her except she is giving a press conference.

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:55.720
So they all jump in this little car and go
screaming through the streets of London to

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:02.629
get to the hotel where the press conference
is so he can get there and declare his love

00:07:02.629 --> 00:07:04.669
for her before she leaves town.

00:07:04.669 --> 00:07:11.050
That ticking clock speeds up the momentum,
accelerates the pace, raises the level of

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:16.880
conflict and puts a greater obstacle for the
hero to overcome and that makes the structure

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:17.880
more effective.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.880
Question for the Viewers: What are some great
examples of “Superior Position” and “Ticking

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:18.887
Clocks” in a TV show or Movie?

